---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';

const path = '/inches/';

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How many sixteenths are in an inch?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Sixteen. The on-screen inch ruler divides every inch into 16 equal ticks. The 8th tick is the half inch, the 4th and 12th are quarter inches, and the odd-numbered eighths (2nd, 6th, 10th, 14th) are eighth-inch marks.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How many centimeters is an inch?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Exactly 2.54 centimeters, by international definition. That makes one centimeter about 0.3937 inches.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Why do rulers use fractions instead of decimals?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Tradition and divisibility. Halves, quarters, and eighths come from repeatedly halving an inch, which is easy to do physically and to read by eye. Decimal inches exist too — machinists use thousandths — but fractional inches remain the standard for everyday US measuring.',
      },
    },
  ],
};

const related = [
  { href: '/cm/', label: 'Centimeter ruler' },
  { href: '/mm/', label: 'Millimeter ruler' },
  { href: '/guide/', label: 'How to read the ruler' },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Online Inch Ruler — Measure in Inches at True Size | Real Online Ruler"
  description="A free on-screen inch ruler at true physical size with fractional ticks down to 1/16 inch. Calibrate once, then measure in whole and fractional inches."
  path={path}
  h1="Inch ruler"
  lede="The inch remains the everyday unit across the United States — for woodworking, sewing, hardware, and anything sold by the foot. Calibrate your display once, switch the ruler to inches, and read whole inches plus fractions down to a sixteenth."
  breadcrumb="Inch ruler"
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}
  related={related}
>
  <h2>What is an inch?</h2>
  <p>
    An inch is a US customary (imperial) unit defined as <strong>exactly 2.54 centimeters</strong>.
    Twelve inches make a foot, 36 make a yard. Unlike metric units, inches are traditionally read as{' '}
    <strong>fractions</strong> — halves, quarters, eighths, sixteenths — rather than decimals, and
    the on-screen ruler is drawn exactly that way.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the inch scale</h2>
  <p>
    Numbered ticks are whole inches. Between them, <strong>tick length encodes the fraction</strong>{' '}
    — the longer the tick, the simpler the fraction:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Longest unnumbered tick</strong> — the half inch (½″), one per inch.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Next longest</strong> — quarter inches (¼″, ¾″).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Medium ticks</strong> — eighth inches (⅛″, ⅜″, ⅝″, ⅞″).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Shortest ticks</strong> — sixteenths (1/16″ … 15/16″).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Read from the numbered inch below the object's end and count forward. Two medium ticks past the
    3″ mark is 3 and 2/8 — simplify it to <strong>3¼″</strong>. Five of the shortest ticks past 1″
    is 1 and 5/16 inches. The full reading method, with worked examples, is in the
    <a href="/guide/">reading guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Fraction ↔ decimal ↔ metric</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Fraction</th>
        <th>Decimal (in)</th>
        <th>Metric</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>1/16″</td>
        <td>0.0625</td>
        <td>1.59 mm</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>⅛″</td>
        <td>0.125</td>
        <td>3.18 mm</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>¼″</td>
        <td>0.25</td>
        <td>6.35 mm</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>½″</td>
        <td>0.5</td>
        <td>12.7 mm</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>1″</td>
        <td>1.0</td>
        <td>25.4 mm (exact)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>When inches beat metric</h2>
  <p>
    If the thing you're measuring was <em>made</em> in inches — lumber dimensions, screw sizes, pipe
    fittings, US clothing patterns — measure in inches and skip the conversion. A "2×4" stud, a ¼″
    bolt, or a 9″ cake pan all have round numbers in inches and awkward ones in metric. Match the
    ruler to the object's native unit and the numbers stay friendly.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How many sixteenths are in an inch?</h3>
  <p>
    Sixteen. The on-screen inch ruler divides every inch into 16 equal ticks. The 8th tick is the
    half inch, the 4th and 12th are quarter inches, and the 2nd, 6th, 10th, and 14th are eighth-inch
    marks.
  </p>
  <h3>How many centimeters is an inch?</h3>
  <p>
    Exactly 2.54 centimeters, by international definition. One centimeter is about 0.3937 inches.
  </p>
  <h3>Why do rulers use fractions instead of decimals?</h3>
  <p>
    Tradition and divisibility. Halves, quarters, and eighths come from repeatedly halving an inch,
    which is easy to do physically and to read by eye. Decimal inches exist too — machinists work in
    thousandths — but fractional inches remain the standard for everyday US measuring.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
